{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-219563","model_call":57,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ free \"no skin in the game\" value)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ free \"no skin in the game\" value)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_282584169#4_641616581","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3926","rank":1,"snippet":"CRE? Broker Opinion of Value vs. Appraisals How a Broker Opinion of Value Comes Together An Example of a BOV used in CRE Using Property Intelligence to Collect Insights for a BOV \" , \"segment\": \"Appraisals are put together by licensed third-party professionals who have <cover>no “skin” in the game, as it pertains to the deal at hand. 2. A BOV is essentially a broker’s “best guess” at a property’s value, albeit a “best guess” backed by market data and other industry knowledge. The guidelines for appraisals are much more stringent, including more robust data collection and analysis. That said, a skilled broker with access to up-to-date market information can create a BOV as thorough as a commercial MAI certified appraiser. 3. Appraisers will always charge a fee for conducting an appraisal. BOVs are often put together by brokers for free, </cover>especially if trying to win over a client to get a listing. 4. BOVs can be used to determine the value of an entire portfolio of CRE assets, whereas appraisals are generally property-specific.\" , \"start_char\": 2333.000000 , \"title\":"},"time":137},"model_calls":57,"document_judgments":35,"relevance_3":1,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
